(registered 2026-10-29, last updated 2026-10-29)

Media type name: application

Media subtype name: vnd.idop+zip

Required parameters: N/A

Optional parameters: N/A

Encoding considerations: binary

Security considerations: An IDOP package is a ZIP archive that 
contains active content: HTML, CSS, JavaScript and WebAssembly under 
the "code/" root, which a conforming Reader executes. The published 
specification is organised around this fact (Sections 4-12 and 17).

Validation before execution. A Reader validates the complete package
before any of its code runs: ZIP structure, entry names, size, 
entry-count and compression-ratio limits, the manifest, and a static
executable-boundary policy. It refuses the whole package on any failure.
The static policy is defence in depth: code can reach the constructs 
it forbids indirectly, so the isolation below is the security 
boundary.

Isolation. Document code runs in an isolated execution context. In web
browsers this is a sandboxed iframe on an opaque origin, with a Content
Security Policy that permits no network connections, and a Permissions
Policy that denies device and payment features. The context has no access
to the Reader, its storage or credentials, or other documents. Dedicated
workers inherit the same isolation; shared and service workers are
refused. Some channels are not governed by a Content Security Policy in
current browser engines: WebRTC (STUN/TURN over UDP and the DNS lookups
it causes), nested "srcdoc" frames and speculative loading such as
<link rel="prerender">. The specification requires a Reader to close them
before document code runs (Section 9.3); the reference Reader removes the
WebRTC interfaces from the document's realm and refuses, synchronously,
to create or insert frames and speculative links to remote addresses.

Data paths. Network access exists only as a capability the user grants,
to exact HTTPS origins, through a Reader-side broker. The broker does not
follow redirects, bounds request and response size, time and the number
of requests, and injects credentials the document can never read. No
interface returns a secret. Files enter or leave a document only through
Reader dialogs the user confirms. Data can also leave without such a
dialog in three ways tied to the user: text written to the clipboard
after a user gesture, the document's state saved into its own file after
a user gesture, and automatic saving into a revision-keeping location the
user opened the document from.

Processing outside a Reader. Any ZIP tool can extract a package. If the
extracted entry point is opened directly in a web browser, or the entries
are served from a web origin, the code runs with that context's
privileges and none of the protections above apply. Software that does
not implement the specification should treat the type as opaque data and
not execute or render its entries. SVG and similar resources can contain
script; they are passive only as a Reader serves them.

Container and parser risks. The ZIP profile forbids ZIP64, encryption,
multi-volume archives, compression methods other than Store and Deflate,
bytes before the first entry or after the archive, bytes the central
directory does not account for, special entries, and disagreement between
local and central headers. Path rules prevent traversal and name
collisions on case-insensitive file systems (Unicode full case folding).
Limits on total size, per-entry size, entry count and compression ratio
bound decompression attacks. The security considerations of ZIP as a
structured syntax (RFC 6839, Section 3.6) apply. The manifest is JSON
(RFC 8259; the considerations of its Section 12 apply), validated against
a published JSON Schema with duplicate keys refused and size, depth and
node limits.

Integrity, authenticity and confidentiality. The format provides no
integrity protection beyond ZIP CRC-32 values, which detect accidental
damage only, and no authenticity: metadata such as title and author is
self-declared, and packages are neither signed nor encrypted in this
version. Saved state is stored in plain form, readable by anyone who
holds the file. Integrity and confidentiality in transit and at rest must
be provided externally, for example by TLS.

External references. Interpreting a package does not require
dereferencing any external link.

Residual risks. Defects in the hosting browser engine; egress channels of
an engine that neither its Content Security Policy nor the Reader's
measures close, including ones added by future web platform features;
side channels; denial of service through CPU or memory use by running
code; and social engineering, since a document can display anything,
including imitations of a Reader's or another site's interface.

Interoperability considerations: The container is a ZIP archive 
(PKWARE APPNOTE) restricted to the Store and Deflate methods, with no
ZIP64. Its first entry is a Stored "mimetype" entry without an extra 
field, as in OpenDocument and EPUB, so the media type sits at a fixed 
offset. Some general-purpose ZIP tools add extra fields or reorder 
entries by default; a package re-created with such a tool is usually 
refused by conforming Readers. Entry names are UTF-8, normalised to 
NFC, and restricted so that they extract safely on common file 
systems. The manifest is JSON validated by a published JSON Schema. 
Minor versions (1.x) only add features; a Reader refuses a package 
that uses members or required features it does not implement, so a 
file written for a later 1.x may be refused by an earlier Reader. A 
new major version will use a new media type. A public conformance 
suite lists packages that must be opened and packages that must be 
refused.

Published specification: IDOP Format Specification 1.0, IDOP LABS.
This version: https://idoplabs.com/spec/idop/1.0/2026-10-13/
Latest version: https://idoplabs.com/spec/idop/1.0/
JSON Schema: https://idoplabs.com/spec/idop/1.0/schemas/manifest.json
Conformance suite: https://idoplabs.com/spec/idop/1.0/conformance/

Applications which use this media: IDOP Cloud 
(https://cloud.idoplabs.com), a browser-based Reader; the "idop" 
command-line validator and packer 
(https://idoplabs.com/developers/sdk/); and other software that 
produces IDOP documents, including AI assistants.

Fragment identifier considerations: No fragment identifier syntax is 
defined for this type. In line with the +zip structured syntax suffix 
(RFC 6839, Section 3.6), which defines none for application/zip, 
fragment identifiers have no meaning for this type.

Restrictions on usage: N/A

Additional information:

1. Deprecated alias names for this type: N/A
2. Magic number(s): "PK" 0x03 0x04 at offset 0; the ASCII string 
"mimetype" at offset 30; the ASCII string "application/vnd.idop+zip" 
at offset 38.
3. File extension(s): .idop
4. Macintosh file type code: N/A
5. Object Identifiers: N/A

General Comments: The "+zip" suffix follows RFC 6839; the file is an 
ordinary ZIP archive.

Uniform Type Identifier used on Apple platforms (informative):
com.idoplabs.idop, conforming to public.zip-archive. Pre-release files
(format versions 0.1-draft and 0.2-draft) carried the unregistered 
label "application/vnd.idop.foundation+zip"; they are not instances 
of this type.

